__d(function(e,a,i,t,n,s,o){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2013 History",textbook:"Our Pasts \u2013 I",chapter_number:7,chapter_title:"New Questions and Ideas",level_1_mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does the word 'Buddha' mean?",options:["The great king","The awakened/enlightened one","The first teacher","The one who suffers"],correct_answer:"The awakened/enlightened one",explanation:"'Buddha' means 'the awakened one' or 'the enlightened one'. Siddhartha Gautama received this title after attaining enlightenment (bodhi) under the peepal tree at Bodhgaya."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Where did Siddhartha Gautama attain enlightenment?",options:["Sarnath","Lumbini","Bodhgaya","Vaishali"],correct_answer:"Bodhgaya",explanation:"Siddhartha Gautama attained enlightenment under a peepal (Bodhi) tree at Bodhgaya in Bihar. Lumbini is his birthplace; Sarnath is where he gave his first sermon."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Where did the Buddha give his first sermon?",options:["Bodhgaya","Deer Park, Sarnath","Vaishali","Rajagriha"],correct_answer:"Deer Park, Sarnath",explanation:"The Buddha gave his first sermon at the Deer Park in Sarnath, near Varanasi. This event is called the Dhammachakkapavattana Sutta \u2014 'Setting the Wheel of Dhamma in motion'."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which is the FIRST of the Buddha's Four Noble Truths?",options:["Desire is the cause of suffering","There is a path to end suffering","Suffering exists in life (Dukkha)","Suffering can be ended"],correct_answer:"Suffering exists in life (Dukkha)",explanation:"The First Noble Truth is Dukkha \u2014 the recognition that suffering exists in life. Old age, sickness, death, and unfulfilled desire are all forms of dukkha. This is the starting point of Buddhist philosophy."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"According to the Buddha, what causes suffering?",options:["Birth into a lower varna","Craving and desire (tanha)","Lack of Vedic knowledge","Not performing rituals"],correct_answer:"Craving and desire (tanha)",explanation:"The Second Noble Truth (Samudaya) teaches that suffering is caused by tanha \u2014 craving or desire. The attachment to things, people, and outcomes \u2014 and the fear of losing them \u2014 generates suffering."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the 'Middle Way' in Buddhism?",options:["A road between two cities","Avoiding both extreme luxury and extreme self-denial","A compromise between Hinduism and Jainism","Teaching in both Sanskrit and Prakrit"],correct_answer:"Avoiding both extreme luxury and extreme self-denial",explanation:"The Middle Way (Madhyama Pratipad) is the Buddha's teaching to avoid two extremes: living in extreme luxury (as he did as a prince) and practising extreme self-denial (harsh asceticism). A balanced, moderate life is the correct path."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Mahavira was the ________ Tirthankara of Jainism.",options:["First","Twelfth","Twenty-third","Twenty-fourth"],correct_answer:"Twenty-fourth",explanation:"Mahavira was the 24th and last Tirthankara of Jainism. A Tirthankara is a 'ford-maker' \u2014 one who has crossed the ocean of rebirth and shows others the path. The first Tirthankara is Rishabhanatha."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does 'ahimsa' mean?",options:["Sacrifice to the gods","Non-violence","Renunciation of the world","Truthfulness"],correct_answer:"Non-violence",explanation:"Ahimsa means non-violence \u2014 the principle of not harming any living being through action, speech, or thought. It is central to both Buddhism and Jainism, and was later adopted by Mahatma Gandhi."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is NOT one of Mahavira's five vows?",options:["Ahimsa (non-violence)","Satya (truth)","Dana (gift-giving)","Aparigraha (non-possession)"],correct_answer:"Dana (gift-giving)",explanation:"Mahavira's five vows are: Ahimsa (non-violence), Satya (truth), Asteya (non-stealing), Brahmacharya (celibacy), and Aparigraha (non-possession). Dana (gift-giving) is a practice in other religious traditions but is not one of Mahavira's five vows."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"In which clan was Mahavira born?",options:["Shakya clan","Licchavi clan of Vajji","Nanda dynasty","Maurya clan"],correct_answer:"Licchavi clan of Vajji",explanation:"Mahavira (Vardhamana) was born into the Licchavi clan of the Vajji confederacy in modern Bihar. The Buddha, by contrast, was born into the Shakya clan in Lumbini (modern Nepal)."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What are the Upanishads primarily concerned with?",options:["Hymns praising Vedic gods","Philosophical questions about the self and ultimate reality","Instructions for performing sacrifices","Epic stories of gods and heroes"],correct_answer:"Philosophical questions about the self and ultimate reality",explanation:"The Upanishads are philosophical texts (800\u2013200 BCE) that moved away from Vedic ritual and explored deeper questions: What is the self (atman)? What is ultimate reality (Brahman)? How does one attain liberation? They argued inner knowledge, not ritual, leads to liberation."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Who was Gargi? Why is she significant?",options:["A Jain queen who built temples","A female Upanishadic philosopher who debated with great sages","The mother of the Buddha","A Vedic singer who composed hymns"],correct_answer:"A female Upanishadic philosopher who debated with great sages",explanation:"Gargi Vachaknavi was a female philosopher mentioned in the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad who engaged in philosophical debates with the sage Yajnavalkya in the royal court. She is historically significant as proof that women participated in intellectual life in ancient India."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why did the Buddha teach in Prakrit instead of Sanskrit?",options:["He did not know Sanskrit","To make his teachings accessible to all people, not just brahmin scholars","Sanskrit was banned in his time","Prakrit was the language of priests"],correct_answer:"To make his teachings accessible to all people, not just brahmin scholars",explanation:"The Buddha deliberately chose to teach in Prakrit \u2014 the language of ordinary people \u2014 so his message could be understood by everyone, regardless of varna or Vedic education. This was a democratic choice to bypass brahmin monopoly on sacred language."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the most important Jain pilgrimage site in Karnataka?",options:["Hampi","Sringeri","Shravanabelagola","Badami"],correct_answer:"Shravanabelagola",explanation:"Shravanabelagola in Hassan district, Karnataka, is the most important Jain pilgrimage site in South India. It houses the 57-foot monolithic statue of Gommateshwara (Bahubali), erected in 981 CE by the Western Ganga dynasty."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What does the word 'Tirthankara' mean?",options:["River-crosser","Ford-maker (one who helps others cross the ocean of rebirth)","Ritual performer","Royal teacher"],correct_answer:"Ford-maker (one who helps others cross the ocean of rebirth)",explanation:"'Tirthankara' literally means 'ford-maker' \u2014 one who has crossed the ocean of rebirth (samsara) and creates a ford (crossing-point) for others to follow. There are 24 Tirthankaras in Jainism, with Mahavira as the 24th."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What are the 'Three Jewels' (Triratna) of Buddhism?",options:["Gold, silver, and bronze","Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha","Ahimsa, Satya, and Karma","Brahmin, Kshatriya, and Vaishya"],correct_answer:"Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha",explanation:"The Three Jewels (Triratna) of Buddhism are: Buddha (the teacher), Dhamma (the teachings/Four Noble Truths), and Sangha (the community of monks, nuns, and lay followers). Taking refuge in all three is the core of becoming a Buddhist."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Easy",question:"How many steps are there in the Eightfold Path?",options:["Four","Six","Eight","Ten"],correct_answer:"Eight",explanation:"The Eightfold Path (Arya Ashtangika Marga) has eight steps: Right Understanding, Right Intention, Right Speech, Right Action, Right Livelihood, Right Effort, Right Mindfulness, and Right Concentration."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following did BOTH the Buddha and Mahavira have in common?",options:["Both were born in the same city","Both rejected birth-based caste discrimination and opposed expensive rituals","Both were vegetarians who prohibited all animal products","Both composed Vedic hymns"],correct_answer:"Both rejected birth-based caste discrimination and opposed expensive rituals",explanation:"Both the Buddha and Mahavira: (1) accepted followers of all varnas into their communities; (2) opposed expensive brahmin rituals and animal sacrifice; (3) taught in Prakrit; (4) emphasised inner virtue over birth-based identity. These commonalities made both traditions revolutionary."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is 'nirvana' in Buddhism?",options:["A state of extreme asceticism","Liberation \u2014 the extinguishing of desire leading to freedom from rebirth","A type of meditation posture","The name for the Buddhist sacred text"],correct_answer:"Liberation \u2014 the extinguishing of desire leading to freedom from rebirth",explanation:"Nirvana literally means 'extinguishing' \u2014 the complete extinguishing of desire, hatred, and delusion. It is the state of liberation from the cycle of birth, death, and rebirth (samsara) \u2014 the ultimate goal of Buddhist practice."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why did the questioning of Vedic rituals begin around 600 BCE?",options:["Because the Vedas were lost","Because growing cities, trade, and social complexity created new questions about life, equality, and justice","Because Sanskrit became a forgotten language","Because priests had no more rituals to perform"],correct_answer:"Because growing cities, trade, and social complexity created new questions about life, equality, and justice",explanation:"The Mahajanapada era brought urbanisation, trade, and social change. The growing merchant class, urban poor, and diverse communities began questioning the value of expensive rituals, birth-based discrimination, and brahmin authority \u2014 creating fertile ground for new ideas."}],common_misconceptions:[{misconception:"The Buddha said life is only suffering and one should be miserable.",why_confused:"Students hear 'the First Noble Truth is suffering' and assume Buddhism is pessimistic.",correct_explanation:"Buddhism is ultimately OPTIMISTIC. The First Noble Truth acknowledges that suffering exists \u2014 but the Third Noble Truth says it CAN END, and the Fourth provides the path to end it. The Buddha's message was one of hope: suffering has a cause, and removing that cause removes the suffering. Nirvana \u2014 complete peace \u2014 is attainable.",mini_practice_question:"Is Buddhism a pessimistic religion? Justify with reference to the Four Noble Truths.",answer:"No. While the First Noble Truth acknowledges suffering (Dukkha), the Third Noble Truth (Nirodha) says suffering CAN END, and the Fourth (Magga) provides the Eightfold Path to end it. Buddhism is ultimately optimistic \u2014 it offers liberation."},{misconception:"Buddhism and Jainism are the same religion because both teach ahimsa.",why_confused:"Sharing the principle of ahimsa makes students assume both are identical.",correct_explanation:"Buddhism and Jainism share ahimsa but differ significantly: (1) Jainism's ahimsa is absolute \u2014 every living being (including plants, microorganisms) has a soul and must not be harmed. Buddhism's ahimsa is important but practised through the Middle Way. (2) Jainism believes in permanent individual souls (jiva); Buddhism is more agnostic about a permanent self. (3) The paths to liberation differ: Jainism emphasises extreme asceticism; Buddhism teaches the Middle Way. (4) Mahavira is the 24th Tirthankara; the Buddha is not a Tirthankara.",mini_practice_question:"Name TWO differences between Buddhist and Jain teachings.",answer:"1. Jain ahimsa extends to ALL living beings including plants/insects; Buddhist ahimsa follows the Middle Way and is less extreme. 2. Jainism requires extreme asceticism (fasting severely); Buddhism teaches the Middle Way avoiding both extreme self-denial and extreme luxury."}]}},2733,[]);